.TH CEVENTLOOP_CONSTRUCT 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ceventloop_construct \- declare and initialise a ceventloop in one step
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ceventloop_construct(name, max_events_per_wait, num_lock_stripes,
                     num_reactor_threads);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a ceventloop variable called name and initialises it
with ceventloop_create(). If the create fails, the macro calls
_ccol_fatal_err().
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name                  Name of the variable for the ceventloop handle.
max_events_per_wait   Size of the polling thread's event batch buffer,
                      from 1 to INT_MAX. It must also be small enough
                      that max_events_per_wait times the size of one
                      kernel event record (struct epoll_event on Linux,
                      struct kevent on FreeBSD) fits in a size_t.
num_lock_stripes      Number of independent lock stripes for the
                      registry of fds and entries, at least 1. With 1
                      there is a single shared lock.
num_reactor_threads   Number of background reactor threads, at least 1.
                      With 1, a single thread both polls and dispatches
                      the callbacks in line.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Because a failure ends in _ccol_fatal_err(), call
ceventloop_create() or ceventloop_create_mp() directly when
you want to handle the error yourself.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ceventloop_construct(loop, 32, 1, 1);
ceventloop_reg reg = ceventloop_add(loop, cselectable_fd(fd, cselect_read),
                                    handlers, NULL, NULL);
ceventloop_remove(loop, reg);
ceventloop_destroy(loop);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ceventloop_construct_scoped (3),
.BR ceventloop_declare (3),
.BR ceventloop_create (3),
.BR ceventloop_add (3),
.BR ceventloop_destroy (3)
